in the early hours of Saturday an  announcement that Sudan's military junta  and the opposition coalition had agreed  on how to govern the country they  fleshed out the details on the makeup  and functions of a transitional  government until elections in three  years I'm very pleased in the name of  the African mediation and two respected  delegations to declare to the Sudanese  people and the international community  that the two delegations have fully  agreed on the constitutional project the  reaction was immediate on the streets of  Khartoum announcing the deal African  Union in vuoi Muhammad Hassan labott  gave no detail about the contents of the  declaration he said talks are continuing  on how and when it would be signed  during the negotiations the military  council ceded two demands for justice  over the killing of at least six people  including four schoolchildren at a  protest earlier in the week nine  soldiers were detained it was a victory  for tens of thousands of Sudanese who  had marched demanding justice for the  deaths it was certainly a horrible  massacre we pray the situation improves  and that the right of the students are  not neglected blood for blood and  punishment is a must  [Applause]  protesters have been flexing their  muscles since December  frustration started with the economy  President Omar al-bashir was in their  crosshairs the number of protests grew  along with their demands until Bashir  was removed in the military coup in  April general setup a military council  the protesters demanded civilians take  the lead in the transition they started  a sitting outside the army headquarters  forcing military rulers to begin talks  over how to share power on June 3rd  security forces raid at that  demonstration they killed more than 100  people and injured many more talks  resumed a month later mediated by the  African Union in Ethiopia the first  breakthrough came on July 17 leading to  the signing of a political agreement  between the military junta and the  opposition coalition they agreed on an  11 person transitional council with  representatives from both sides and a  rotating leadership the disagreements  had remained over the wording of  constitutional changes now with the  details agreed and assigning eminent  Sudanese people have a roadmap to a more  peaceful future Shalit Bellis al Jazeera
 

